# Abstract

While \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) represents one of the most promising catalysts for soot oxidation, conventional \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles (NPs) tend to aggregate, losing their activities. Herein, an alternative approach is proposed for preparing three-dimensional nanostructured \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (NSCo) using the hierarchically-structured Co-based Metal Organic Frameworks as a precursor. Specifically, ZIF-67 is chosen as the precursor as ZIF-67 can be conveniently synthesized with high yields and it can be easily converted to NSCo via calcination. The resulting NSCo exhibits a unique morphology which enables NSCo to possess more porosities and surface areas than the typical \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs. Consequently, NSCo shows a much higher catalytic activity than the typical \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs for soot oxidation because of superior textural properties of NSCo. Besides, when the soot oxidation by the typical \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs produced a significant amount of unwanted CO, soot can be completely combusted into \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) using NSCo. In comparison with other reported Co-related catalysts, NSCo also achieves a higher soot oxidation efficiency (100% conversion) at lower temperatures with \(\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{ig}}\) of \(331\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). NSCo can be reused over many continuous cycles and still retains its catalytic activities. These features validate that NSCo is an easy-to-prepare 3D nanostructured \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) catalyst, which possesses advantageous capabilities for soot oxidation at lower temperatures.

Keywords: MOFs, \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\), soot oxidation, carbon black, catalytic oxidation
# 1. Introduction

While diesel engines have been extensively employed owing to their high fuel efficacies, low cost of operation as well as high durability, an unfavorable by-product, soot, from diesel engines would cause adverse effects on public health and the environment. As catalytic oxidation of soot integrated with diesel particulate filters is recognized as the most useful approach for soot elimination [1-4], development of highly active catalysts for soot oxidation has been extremely critical for efficient soot elimination [1].

To date, many metal species and metal oxides have been proposed as catalysts for soot oxidation, including Ag, Ce, Co, Mn, La, etc. [5-12] Among these metals, Co, especially \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\), appears as one of the most promising catalysts for soot oxidation because \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) requires relatively low activation energy for easy release of its lattice oxygen, making \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) an attractive catalyst for catalytic combustion of methane, hydrocarbons, CO, and ammonia [13]. To date, many \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)-related catalysts have been developed for soot oxidation, these catalysts are generally designed by two strategies via either mediating contact interfaces between catalysts and soot or escalating intrinsic activities of catalysts [14, 15]. As the catalyst-soot contact plays a fundamental role in soot combustion owing to the fact that soot oxidation is a gas-solid-solid reaction, many attempts have been made to modulate structures and morphologies of solid catalysts to improve the catalyst-soot contact [14, 15]. \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) has been fabricated into nanoparticles (NPs) to increase its surface area for maximizing its contact with soot [16, 17]. However, typical \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs could be easily agglomerated [18, 19], and then exposed very low surface areas to soot particulates, thereby decreasing their catalytic activities [17]. Hence, it is highly desirable to develop \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)-based catalysts which can be configured
into well-defined and hierarchical structures to prevent aggregation of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs [20, 21].

While a few approaches are adopted to develop some specially-shaped \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) materials, such as \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) fibers [15, 16], three-dimensional (3D) ordered mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) [14], etc., these \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) materials require sophisticated and multiple-step fabrications, making them less practical for large-scale production [14-16]. Therefore, alternative approaches are necessitated for developing hierarchically-structured catalysts of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs via more convenient and environmentally benign routes. To this end, this study proposes another strategy to produce such a \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) material from Co-based metal organic frameworks (MOFs). As MOFs consist of metal sites as nodes and organic ligands as bridges connected via hierarchical coordination, MOFs have been validated as advantageous precursors for fabricating 3D hierarchical nanostructures of metal oxides [22-27]. Specially, a Co-based MOF, Zeolitic Imidazolate Framework-67 (ZIF-67), is selected here because ZIF-67 could be easily, quickly, and massively prepared using inexpensive reagents in water/alcohols [28-35].

Through simple thermal treatments, ZIF-67 can be readily converted into 3D nanostructured \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (NSCo), which shall be an advantageous catalyst for soot oxidation over typical \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs for soot oxidation. Such a catalyst has almost never reported for soot oxidation in the literature. Thus, the study aims to develop and investigate this NSCo, derived from Co-MOF, for soot oxidation and compare it with typical \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs.

# 2. Experimental

# 2.1 Materials
All chemicals involved in this study were commercially available and used as received without purification. Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate and H-methylimidazole (H-MIM) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (USA). Commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs (50-80 nm) were purchased from Alfa Aesar (USA). Carbon black pigment branded as Printex \(\textsuperscript{\textregistered}\) U was employed as a model soot and it was supplied by Orion Engineered Carbons (Luxemburg).

# 2.2 Preparation and characterization of NSCo

NSCo was fabricated through thermal treatments of ZIF-67 based on the illustration shown in Fig. 1(a). ZIF-67 was firstly synthesized by mixing \(1.5\ \mathrm{g}\) of cobalt nitrate hexahydrate and \(3\ \mathrm{g}\) of H-MIM in \(40\ \mathrm{mL}\) of methanol. The resulting mixture was then stirred for \(2\ \mathrm{h}\) and left without stirring for \(4\ \mathrm{h}\) at ambient temperature. The resulting precipitate was then collected, washed by methanol, and dried at \(65\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to obtain ZIF-67, which was then carbonized at \(550\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 2 hours in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and subsequently calcined in air at \(550\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for another 2 hours at a rate of \(10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}\) to produce NSCo.

NSCo was then characterized for its morphologies by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (JEOL JEM-2010, Japan). The crystalline structure of NSCo was measured by an X-ray diffractometer (XRD) (Bruker D8, USA). Surface chemistry of NSCo was determined using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (PHI 5000, Japan). Thermogravimetric (TG) curves of Co-MOF, carbonized Co-MOF, and NSCo were analyzed using a TG analyzer (TGA 11000, Instrument Specialists Incorporated, USA). Raman spectroscopic analyses were conducted using a Raman confocal spectroscopy device (Tokyo Instruments Inc., Japan). Textural properties and temperature-programmed reduction (TPR) properties of NSCo were measured by a volumetric gas adsorption analyzer equipped with a TCD detector (Anton Paar
Quantachrome Auto IQ) for obtaining \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption/desorption isotherms and chemisorption of hydrogen.

# 2.3 Temperature-programmed oxidation of soot using catalysts

The catalytic activity of NSCo for soot oxidation was tested using a fixed-bed quartz tube reactor. A standard air gas of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) containing \(20.5\ \mathrm{vol.}\%\ \mathrm{O}_{2}\) passed through a mixture of soot and catalyst \((100\ \mathrm{mg})\) (soot : \(\mathrm{NSCo}=1:9\)) at a flow rate of \(100\ \mathrm{mL\ min^{-1}}\). The temperature profile was programmed initially from room temperature to \(150\ \mathrm{^\circ C}\) for \(0.5\ \mathrm{h}\) to purge out the absorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and hydrocarbons, and then temperature increased from \(150\) to \(700\ \mathrm{^\circ C}\) at a heating rate of \(5\ \mathrm{^\circ C\ min^{-1}}\). To investigate soot oxidation under different contact modes, the catalyst and soot were mixed by a spatula to achieve the loose contact mode and ground in an agate mortar to afford the tight contact mode [36, 37].

The temperatures of the catalyst bed and tubular electric furnace were monitored by K-type thermocouples. Outlet concentrations of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}\) were analyzed by a IR-208 (IR Infrared Industries, USA). The catalytic activity was evaluated by values of \(\mathrm{T_{ig}}\), \(\mathrm{T_{m}}\), \(\mathrm{T}_{50}\), \(\mathrm{T}_{90}\), and \(\mathrm{T_{e}}\). \(\mathrm{T_{ig}}\) represented the ignition temperature of catalytic soot oxidation. \(\mathrm{T}_{50}\) and \(\mathrm{T}_{90}\) were defined as the temperatures at which \(50\%\) and \(90\%\) of the soot were oxidized during the experiment, respectively, whereas \(\mathrm{T_{e}}\) was the temperature at which \(100\%\) of soot conversion was achieved. \(\mathrm{T_{m}}\) was defined as the temperature-programmed oxidation (TPO) temperature at the maximum concentration of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) selectivity \(\left(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{CO2}}\right)\) was defined as follows: \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{CO2}}=\left(\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{CO2}}\left(\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{CO}}+\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{CO2}}\right)\right)\times100\%\), where \(\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{CO2}}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{CO}}\) are the outlet concentration of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}\) respectively, and the \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{mCO2}}\) was defined as the \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{CO2}}\) at \(\mathrm{T_{m}}\). For evaluating effects of \(\mathrm{NO}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in feed gases on soot oxidation, a feed gas comprised of air, \(500\ \mathrm{ppm}\) of \(\mathrm{NO}\), and \(2\ \mathrm{vol\%}\) of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was prepared and tested.
# 3. Results and discussion

# 3.1 Characterization of NSCo

Since NSCo originated from ZIF-67, the morphology of ZIF-67 was first determined in Fig. 1(b) (and Fig. S1(a)). The as-synthesized ZIF-67 exhibited smooth and homogenous texture, with a rhombic dodecahedral shape, which is the typical morphology of ZIF-67 [38].

![]('img_url')

![]('img_url')

Fig. 1. (a) Preparation scheme of MOF-derived NSCo, (b) a TEM image of MOF precursor, and (c) a TEM image of NSCo.
The corresponding XRD pattern of ZIF-67 (Fig. 2(a)) can be also well-indexed according to the literature [38]. These features confirmed that ZIF-67 was successfully formulated. Subsequently, ZIF-67 was carbonized and then calcined to produce a derivative of ZIF-67 (Fig. 1(c) and Fig. S1(b)), which still retained the rhombic dodecahedral morphology but underwent a slight contraction owing to the decomposition of the organic portion of ZIF-67 [39], causing ca. \(6.5\%\) size reduction.

In addition, the originally homogenous texture of ZIF-67 had become heterogeneous as many dark-colored rounded particles appeared on the planes. The size distribution of these fine particles (the inset in Fig. 1(c)) confirmed that these fine particles were nanoscale, ranging from 5 to \(15~\mathrm{nm}\). The EDS analysis (Fig. S2) also confirmed that NSCo was primarily comprised of Co and O without other significant elements. Furthermore, an elemental mapping analysis of NSCo was also conducted as displayed in Fig. S3, displaying that Co and O were evenly distributed over NSCo particles without significant aggregations.

Furthermore, its XRD pattern (Fig. 2(b)) could be well indexed to \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) based on JCPDS # 43-1003, validating that the calcined derivative of ZIF-67 was \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\). Thus, the original Co-based ZIF-67 had been converted into \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs which could be configured into a 3D nanostructure of the rhombic dodecahedron.
![]('img_url')

Fig. 2: XRD patterns of (a) Co-MOF (ZIF-67) and (b) NSCo.

The surface chemistry of NSCo was further characterized using XPS. Fig. 3(a) displays the full-survey spectrum of NSCo, in which significant intensities of Co and O species were observed without any other species. Fig. 3(b) further shows the Co2p core-level spectrum, which was deconvoluted into many peaks at 780.2 and \(795.1~\mathrm{eV}\), derived from to \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\), and 781.8 as well as \(796.9\;\mathrm{eV}\) attributed to \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) [40], which were typical species existed in \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\). Meanwhile, the O1s spectrum (Fig. S4) can be deconvoluted into two peaks at ca. 529.3 and \(531.6\,\mathrm{eV}\), indicating the surface lattice oxygen \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{latt}}\right)\) species and the surface-chemisorbed oxygen \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}\right)\) species (e.g. \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{2-}\), \(\mathrm{O}^{-}\)or \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)), respectively.
![]('img_url')

Fig. 3. XPS analyses of NSCo: (a) full-survey spectrum, and (b) Co2p core-level spectrum.

The textural properties of NSCo were then characterized in Fig. 4(a). The corresponding \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption isotherm could be categorized as the IUPAC type V isotherm with a hysteresis loop, indicating that NSCo contained pores, especially mesopores. The BET surface area of NSCo was calculated as \(36\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) with a pore volume of \(0.25\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). As NSCo was composed of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs, it was essential to compare NSCo with commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs, of which the textural properties are also illustrated in Fig. 4. The amount of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption to the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs was significantly lower than that of NSCo, and the corresponding pore volume (Fig. 4(b)) was also extremely low. The surface area of the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs was merely \(2.8\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) with a very small pore volume of \(0.007\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). These results validated that NSCo certainly exhibited much higher porosities than the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs possibly because the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs were seriously agglomerated as seen in Fig. S5. In contrast, those \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs in NSCo
were even smaller and configured into the special shape of rhombic dodecahedron, resulting in the much higher surface area and pore volume.

![]('img_url')

Fig. 4. Textural properties of NSCo and commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\,\mathrm{NPs}\) : (a) \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption isotherms and (b) pore size distributions.

In addition to the textural properties, Raman spectroscopic analyses of NSCo and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\,\mathrm{NPs}\) were also conducted particularly to probe in their surface oxygen vacancies. Fig. 5(a) firstly shows Raman spectrum of the typical \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\,\mathrm{NPs}\), in which several significant peaks were found at 190, 472, 515, and \(678\;\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\), corresponding to the \(\mathrm{F}^{1}{}_{2\mathrm{g}}\), \(\mathrm{E_{g}}\), \(\mathrm{F}^{2}{}_{2\mathrm{g}}\), and \(\mathrm{A}_{1\mathrm{g}}\) modes of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) [41]. The spectrum of NSCo was almost identical to that of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\,\mathrm{NPs}\), demonstrating almost no disordered structure of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) in NSCo [42]. Nevertheless, the peaks of NSCo showed a slight shift to higher frequencies. For instance, the peak of \(\mathrm{F}^{1}{}_{2\mathrm{g}}\) shifted from 191 to \(193\;\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\), and the peak of \(\mathrm{A}_{1\mathrm{g}}\) shifted from 678 to \(684\;\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) as displayed in Fig. 5(b). As the peaks of \(\mathrm{F}^{1}{}_{2\mathrm{g}}\) and \(\mathrm{A}_{1\mathrm{g}}\) correspond to
the Raman vibration of \(\mathrm{C}^{2+}-\mathrm{O}^{2-}\), and \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}-\mathrm{O}^{2-}\), respectively [43, 44], the blue-shifts of these peaks suggested that NSCo might exhibit more surface oxygen vacancies [45].

![]('img_url')

Fig. 5. Raman spectra of NSCo and Co3O4 NPs: (a) full-range spectra and (b) specific (191-193)(678-682)

In addition to textural properties, NSCo was further compared with the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) via TPR analyses to probe into active species existed in these two materials. Fig. 6(a) shows that the reduction profile of NSCo started gradually from 300 \(^\circ C\) with a noticeable peak at \(400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(480~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), possibly attributed to \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) in NSCo, respectively [17, 46]. On the other hand, the reduction profile of the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) started gradually from \(370\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with peaks at ca. 470 and \(510\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) [17]. These comparisons demonstrated that the Co species in NSCo were much more active than those in the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) even at low temperatures, revealing that NSCo possessed more superior redox properties than the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs.
![]('img_url')

Fig. 6. Characterizations of NSCo: (a) TPR profiles and (b) TG analyses.

![]('img_url')

As NSCo was derived from the thermal treatment of ZIF-67, TGA was then adopted to TG behaviors of ZIF-67 by carbonization and calcination as shown in Fig. 6(b). When ZIF-67 was carbonized, ZIF-67 revealed gradual weight loss from room temperature to \(250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), due to escape of moisture and gas molecules confined within ZIF-67. Next, a huge weight loss occurred owing to decomposition of the organic ligand of ZIF-67, and the weight remained almost unchanged afterwards. The carbonized ZIF-67 was subsequently calcined in air to produce NSCo and the corresponding TG curve of carbonized ZIF-67 displayed a small weight drop before \(200\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Subsequently, the weight of carbonized ZIF-67 dropped to \(49\%\) because of oxidation of carbonaceous components. Besides, the TG of NSCo in air was also measured and it remained stable up to \(800\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), validating the high thermal stability of NSCo, which enables NSCo to be a suitable catalyst for soot oxidation.
# 3.2 Soot oxidation by NSCo and commercial \(\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\) NPs

3.2 Soot oxidation by NSCo and commercial \(\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\) NPsPrior to evaluating catalytic activities of NSCo for soot oxidation, the TPO profile of soot oxidation without any catalysts was measured as a reference line. Fig. 7(a) shows that the \( \mathrm{T_{ig}} \) of soot oxidation in the absence of catalysts was \( 490\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), and \( \mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{CO}2} \) (Fig. 7(b) and (c)) was \( 35.1\% \), demonstrating that soot oxidation in the absence of catalysts required relatively high temperatures and produced the unwanted by-product, CO, during the soot oxidation. Once NSCo was added, the corresponding \( \mathrm{T_{ig}} \) decreased significantly from \( 490\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) to \( 331\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) with \( \mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{CO}2} = 100\% \), indicating that NSCo successfully catalyzed soot oxidation and enhanced the conversion efficiency of soot to \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \).

![]('img_url')

Fig. 7. Soot oxidation by NSCo and commercial \( \mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) NPs : (a) Soot conversion (%) (loose contact mode) (b) CO concentration during soot oxidation and (c) \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) concentration during soot oxidation.
Furthermore, the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs were also employed to catalyze soot oxidation, and soot oxidation was certainly improved as \(\mathrm{T_{ig}}\) was also substantially decreased to \(410\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which, however, was still much higher than the \(\mathrm{T_{ig}}\) by NSCo. This indicated that NSCo showed a higher catalytic activity than \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs for soot oxidation owing to the fact that NSCo exhibited the larger surface area in contact with soot than the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs [47, 48]. Besides, NSCo also possessed a more superior redox property and surface oxygen vacancies than the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs, thereby exhibiting the improved intrinsic activities for soot oxidation [46]. More importantly, while \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NP was able to convert soot completely with \(100\%\) conversion, CO was co-produced with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) during soot oxidation with \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{CO}2}=73.9\%\). In view of no CO produced from soot oxidation by NSCo, NSCo assuredly exhibited more favorable and efficient catalytic activities for soot oxidation than the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs.

To further evaluate the capability of NSCo, Table 1 lists the results of soot oxidation by various Co-based catalysts reported in literatures. In comparison with other reported catalysts, NSCo certainly showed relatively low values of \(\mathrm{T_{ig}}\), \(\mathrm{T}_{10}\), \(\mathrm{T}_{50}\) and \(\mathrm{T}_{90}\). This indicates that NSCo not only outperformed the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs but also many other Co-based catalysts, validating that NSCo was an advantageous and promising catalyst for soot oxidation.
