# 3.3 Soot oxidation by NSCo in tight contact or loose contact conditions

As contact modes between catalysts and soot powder substantially influence soot oxidation behaviors, two different contact modes were then investigated: loose contact and tight contact modes. The loose contact mode represents a more practical and realistic situation for a mixture of catalyst and soot [36, 37], where the morphology of catalysts play crucial roles for probing into nature of catalyst-soot contact. In addition, tight contact modes lead to intimate contacts between soot and catalysts in order to determine intrinsic activities of catalysts without limitations caused by poor soot-catalyst contact [36, 37]. Fig. 8 (a) shows TPO profile of soot oxidation by NSCo
![]('img_url')

Fig. 8. A comparison of Soot oxidation by NSCo between “loose contact” and “tight contact” conditions: (a) soot conversion $(\%)$ and (b) $\tau_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $\mathrm{SmCO}_{2}$.

under the two modes. While \(\mathrm{Sm}_{\mathrm{CO}2}\) (\%) of both two cases were \(100\%\), TPO profile was substantially improved as \(\mathrm{T_{ig}}\) and \(\mathrm{T}_{50}\) under the tight contact mode were 271 and 295 \(^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), respectively, whereas those values under loose contact mode were 331 and \(416\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), respectively.

Moreover, when \(\mathrm{T_{e}}\) under loose contact mode was \(474\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(\mathrm{T_{e}}\) under tight contact mode was only slightly increased from \(\mathrm{T}_{50}=295\) to \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Fig. 8(b) shows that Tm of loose contact mode was noticeably higher than that of tight contact mode even though both contact modes afforded \(\mathrm{Sm}_{\mathrm{CO}2}=100\%\). These results demonstrate that the soot oxidation efficiency by NSCo was significantly more superior under the tight contact mode than the loose contact mode owing to the fact that an intimate interfacial contact between NSCo and soot was achieved under the tight contact mode, whereas the loose contact mode allowed much less contact between two components [52, 53]. Since the tight contact mode can provide more insights into intrinsic catalytic activities of
catalysts, the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs were also tested for soot oxidation under the tight contact mode. In comparison to the soot oxidation under the loose contact shown in Fig. 7(a), \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs certainly exhibited much improved soot oxidation efficiency as \(\mathrm{T}_{50}\) under the tight contact mode was \(404\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which was much lower than \(\mathrm{T}_{50}\) under the loose contact mode. However, \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs were still less effective than NSCo for soot oxidation even under the tight contact mode, validating that NSCo exhibited intrinsically more superior catalytic activity than the commercial \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs for soot oxidation.

# 3.4 Effects of feed gases on soot oxidation by NSCo

Since feed gases usually consist of other components in addition to nitrogen and oxygen, it would be essential to examine the effects of various feed gases on soot oxidation using NSCo. As NO is commonly present in feed gases for soot oxidation, the effect of NO was then investigated. Fig 9(a) displays TPO profiles of soot oxidation with and without addition of NO.

![]('img_url')

Fig. 9. Effects of NO and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) on soot oxidation by NSCo: (a) soot conversion (\%) and (b) \(\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{m}}\) and \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{mCO}_{2}}\).
In the absence of NO, the \( \mathrm{T_{ig}} \) of NSCo was \( 331\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), which was then considerably shifted to \( 282\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) when NO was present in the feed gas. \( \mathrm{T}_{50} \) by NSCo in the absence of NO was \( 416\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), which was also substantially decreased to \( 340\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) when NO was present during the soot oxidation reaction. Additionally, soot conversion reached \( 100\% \) at \( \mathrm{T_{e}} = 394\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) in the presence of NO, whereas \( \mathrm{T_{e}} \) increased to \( 474\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) in the case of no addition of NO. These results indicate that NO seemed to improve soot oxidation using NSCo. This was possibly because the formation of active oxygen initiated by \( \mathrm{NO}_{2} \) in the gas phase, owing to the presence of NO and \( \mathrm{O}_{2} \), thereby accelerating soot oxidation [54, 55]. Besides, a synergy might occur as a result of surface nitrate decomposition, which led to the formation of gas phase \( \mathrm{NO}_{2} \) and desorption of active oxygen. When NO was introduced to soot oxidation using NSCo, nitrate species might form on the Co active sites and deposited on the catalyst surface. The deposited nitrates would then react with soot by the released \( \mathrm{NO}_{2} \) and/or direct react with soot. Furthermore, the deposited \( \mathrm{NO_{x}} \) species initiated the beginning of soot oxidation, and the NO oxidation ability of the catalyst was responsible for the extensive soot oxidation by \( \mathrm{NO} + \mathrm{O}_{2} \), which is accordance with the "\( \mathrm{NO}_{2} \)-assisted" soot combustion, in which the NO reduction by carbon in soot oxidation [56, 57]. This active oxygen played a critical role in the acceleration of soot oxidation, and contributed more than desorbed \( \mathrm{NO}_{2} \) or \( \mathrm{NO}_{2} \) from surface nitrate decomposition to soot oxidation [54, 55].

Moreover, as \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) might also exist in feed gases together with NO, the presence of both NO and \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) was investigated for their effects on soot oxidation. Fig. 9 shows that TPO profile of soot oxidation by NSCo was further improved in the presence of NO and \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) as \( \mathrm{T_{ig}} \) and \( \mathrm{T}_{50} \) were further decreased to \( 252\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), and \( 323\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), respectively. The corresponding \( \mathrm{T_{e}} \) was also shifted to \( 394\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). This phenomenon could be attributed to that the presence of \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) vapor influenced the wetting of catalyst-soot interface and
thus enhanced oxygen mobility and transfer at the catalyst-soot interface. In a previous relevant study, Burachaloo et al.[58] have suggested that OH radicals generated from O atoms and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules in the discharge facilitate the re-oxidization of the metal oxide vacancies generated by the consumption of oxygen, thus favoring the enhancement of the soot oxidation efficiency. On the other hand, Uchisawa et al. have also reported that water vapor play a role in facilitating the decomposition of the highly stable carbon surface species formed by the partial oxidation of the reacting carbon surface by \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)-attack [59]. These results demonstrated that the feed gas containing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) increased the soot combustion activity regardless of the presence of NO, which could be attributed to the fact that \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) may facilitate the decomposition of the highly stable carbon surface species formed by the partial oxidation of the reacting carbon surface by \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)-attack [59]. The \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) vapor might also serve as a co-catalyst in soot oxidation and lead to the formation of oxygenic intermediates (e.g. oxygen complexes, nitric acid or nitrous acid) on soot surface via interactions between carbon and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), thereby enhancing the soot combustion efficiency [49].

